Recorded engine responses, and how they were recorded.

Every file here is ONE run of the module's own Invoke-DockerFixtureScenario
against a REAL engine, with OS7_DOCKER_RECORD set. Nothing in them was written
by hand. The first line of each says where, when and against which engine; each
line after it is one request and the engine's answer, in order. The path is
stored without its /vX.Y prefix and the version used is stored beside it, so a
replay works at whatever version the module negotiates.

Recorded with installer/testing/capture-docker-fixtures.py — run that, do not
edit these.

  engine-29.1.3.jsonl   2026-10-29, the engine OS/7 would ship: docker.io
                        29.1.3-0ubuntu4.1 from the pinned archive
                        (20260921T000000Z), on an installed OS/7 1.0.0.245
                        Headless machine (os7lab bench `ctr`, snapshot
                        ct3-docker), as root. API 1.52, minimum 1.44. 28
                        exchanges, /system/df included.

  engine-29.7.2.jsonl   2026-10-29, Docker Desktop 29.7.2 on the x64 Windows
                        host, asked by the pinned pwsh 7.6.5 inside a container
                        with the socket mounted. API 1.55, minimum 1.40. 27
                        exchanges. /system/df is NOT recorded here, because on a
                        development host it lists every image and container on
                        it, and these files are public.

THE MODULE'S API RANGE IS THESE FILES. Test-DockerModule requires
$script:DockerApiOldest and $script:DockerApiNewest in Docker.psm1 to equal the
oldest and newest API recorded here. To support a new engine: record it, widen
the constant, run the self-test. Either step alone fails.

WHAT IS IN THEM. One container (os7-fixture-probe, alpine:3.20, printing one
stdout and one stderr line) and one volume (os7-fixture-vol), created, started,
started again (304), logged, inspected, restarted, stopped, stopped again (304),
removed, and asked for again (404). Every list request is FILTERED to the probe.
The capture refuses a file that contains this host's user name or a Windows path.

WHAT IS NOT. No TTY container (the raw log format is handled and unrecorded), no
image pull, no build, no exec, no create beyond the probe, no error from a
running-container remove, no compose project.
